From a1b8749cad39b6a9afd6daa04670c37ba9d7a1b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dingkaijian <dingkaijian@shizhuang-inc.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:32:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] google loop: wrap/unwrap omp v1internal {response:...}
 SSE shell
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

omp's antigravity wire calls /v1internal:streamGenerateContent (no
/models/<model>: segment) and nests every chunk as {"response":{...}};
the client reads Ie.response.candidates/finishReason/usageMetadata.

- util.ts: googleV1InternalPath() — shared by handle.ts dispatch and
  native-intercept.ts so wire-classification and URL-shape gate stay in
  lockstep; googlePathKind still classifies it stream-generate. Also
  antigravityFrameView()/rewrapAntigravityFrame() for the RESPONSE shell.
- Prepared.google.v1internal: prepareGoogle reads handle's urlPath and
  passes it through; pickAdapter hands it to createGoogleAdapter.
- adapter-google: parseStream unwraps the response shell before reading
  candidates/promptFeedback/usageMetadata; every rebuilt chunk (edited
  text, thought, finish stub, settle replay, prose rebuild, emit*)
  is re-shelled via wrapObj/wrapFrame. Passthrough frames keep the
  upstream bytes (shell intact), error frames stay top-level.
- plugin.ts: the plugin-lane strip pipe reads google frames through
  antigravityFrameView too — processGoogle (tag filtering, finishReason),
  the SSE terminal diagnostics and googleUsageSample all looked at
  top-level candidates only, so a finished shelled turn was misreported
  as "upstream stream ended before a completion event" and its usage
  never settled. Rebuilds are re-wrapped into the same shell.
- tests: v1internal shell round-trip, edited rebuild re-shell, settle
  keep-replay, top-level error passthrough, plugin pipe terminates a
  shelled stream without the in-band truncation error.
